$ /usr/bin/python3 replay_kda.py
# cwd kda

==============================================================================
KDA graph replay   device=CUDA
  B=2 T=512 H=4 K=64 V=64 C=64
==============================================================================

1. COLD  (first call, includes compile) :     99,019 ms
2. WARM  (rebuild Python graph each call):     20,506 ms
3. BUILD only (no realize)              :      4,580 ms
   => schedule + launch + execute       :     15,926 ms

4. REPLAY attempt -- build the graph once, then re-dispatch
   graph built and realized: 20,506 ms
   second call, new inputs, rebuild      :     20,282 ms
   same inputs twice -> identical out   : True
   new inputs      -> different out     : True

build / total = 22.3% of the warm call
GPU time      = ~109 ms, i.e. 0.53% of the warm call

DECOMPOSITION of the 20.5 s warm call:
    4.58 s   Python constructing UOps
   15.82 s   TinyGrad's SCHEDULER (rewrite passes)
    0.11 s   GPU execution (measured separately in perf_kda.py)

So it is NOT '99% Python'. It is mostly SCHEDULER.
The kernels ARE compiled once -- COLD is 49.5 s and WARM does not
recompile -- but tinygrad re-runs every rewrite pass on every
realize(), because there is no separation between 'compile' and
'execute' in the API. A replay path that cached the SCHEDULED
PROGRAM LIST, not just the compiled kernels, would bring the per-call
cost down to the 109 ms of GPU time.

